# --- environment ---
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.env
.DS_Store
.hypothesis/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# --- regenerable build products -------------------------------------------------
# `bash app/scripts/replay_demo.sh` rebuilds every one of these in ~35 s, offline, and the
# runs are deterministic: the same seed replays to the same ledger head hash, which a
# test asserts. Committing generated evidence would mean someone comparing a snapshot
# against a rerun and finding two sources of truth, so only the FIVE documents the ITU
# architecture actually names are committed:
#     docs/architecture.md        docs/limits.md          (hand-written)
#     docs/gap_register.md        docs/y3172_map.md       docs/ai_readiness_map.md
traces/
outputs/
docs/kb_report.md
docs/retrieval_eval.md
docs/adviser_eval.md
docs/demo_report.md
docs/failure_atlas.md
docs/source_check.md
docs/run_stats.md
docs/rubric_status.html

# data/clauses/ is entirely a BUILD PRODUCT. `python app/scripts/build_kb.py` regenerates
# clauses.jsonl and chunks.jsonl byte-identically in ~4 s from the three inputs that ARE
# committed: data/sources/clause_seed.jsonl and the two gazette extracts under
# data/sources/IN/. We commit sources, not derivatives.
data/chroma/
data/clauses/

# Run ledgers. NOT committed, and this is safe because the runs are deterministic:
# the same seed replays to the same head hash, which is asserted by a test. Anyone
# regenerates all 11 in ~30 s with `bash app/scripts/replay_demo.sh`. What IS committed is
# outputs/audit_sample.jsonl — the first 50 entries of the India run, which verify as a
# hash chain on their own. 13 MB of regenerable JSONL does not belong in a submission.
runs/

# --- third-party documents -------------------------------------------------------
# Primary source PDFs, fetched by app/scripts/fetch_sources.py and pinned by sha256 in
# data/sources/MANIFEST.yaml. Not redistributed: several have licence terms we have
# not cleared, and all are re-fetchable from their official URL.
kb_raw/


# Throwaway: the tamper demo writes a deliberately corrupted copy of a ledger here.
outputs/tamper/

# --- web UI ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Next.js frontend renders artefacts the Python pipeline wrote; it computes nothing
# of its own.
#
# app/web/public/data/ IS committed, and it is the one deliberate exception to "we commit
# sources, not derivatives". Vercel builds from this repository, so without the bundle in git
# there is nothing to deploy. The exception is safe because the export is byte-for-byte
# reproducible -- the bundle carries the run's head hash instead of a wall-clock stamp -- and
# CI regenerates it on every push and fails if a single byte differs. So it cannot become a
# second source of truth: it is either identical to a fresh run, or the build is red.
app/web/node_modules/
app/web/.next/
app/web/out/
app/web/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo
app/web/next-env.d.ts
# Re-written by `next dev` on every start; committing it only creates churn.
app/web/AGENTS.md
